WEBVTT

NOTE English explanation-card translations. Original component labels remain in Chinese.

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.900 line:5% position:50% size:92% align:center
Electric-double-layer gating
Why can the same channel control need a lower gate voltage?

2
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:07.486 line:5% position:50% size:92% align:center
Gate voltage creates a field through the gate dielectric.

3
00:00:07.486 --> 00:00:12.124 line:5% position:50% size:92% align:center
Holes move away; electrons enter the surface region from source and drain.

4
00:00:12.124 --> 00:00:17.452 line:5% position:50% size:92% align:center
Above threshold, an electron inversion layer forms a conducting channel.

5
00:00:17.452 --> 00:00:22.262 line:5% position:50% size:92% align:center
With source–drain bias: electrons move S → D; conventional current flows D → S.

6
00:00:22.262 --> 00:00:26.728 line:5% position:50% size:92% align:center
Gate voltage controls the channel; source–drain voltage drives transport.

7
00:00:26.728 --> 00:00:31.366 line:5% position:50% size:92% align:center
Opposite gate and channel charges, separated by a dielectric: a capacitor.

8
00:00:31.366 --> 00:00:36.176 line:5% position:50% size:92% align:center
Q = CV. In the parallel-plate picture, C = εA/d: smaller separation, larger capacitance.

9
00:00:36.176 --> 00:00:40.297 line:5% position:50% size:92% align:center
For the same induced charge change: ΔV = ΔQ/C.

10
00:00:40.297 --> 00:00:43.728 line:5% position:50% size:92% align:center
A larger effective gate capacitance means less voltage is needed.

11
00:00:43.728 --> 00:00:48.366 line:5% position:50% size:92% align:center
Replace the solid gate dielectric with an ionic liquid or ion gel.

12
00:00:48.366 --> 00:00:53.348 line:5% position:50% size:92% align:center
Positive and negative ions can move. The unbiased electrolyte is approximately neutral.

13
00:00:53.348 --> 00:00:59.193 line:5% position:50% size:92% align:center
Bias redistributes ions: anions approach the gate; cations approach the semiconductor.

14
00:00:59.193 --> 00:01:03.831 line:5% position:50% size:92% align:center
Cations stay on the electrolyte side, with induced electrons on the semiconductor side.

15
00:01:03.831 --> 00:01:08.469 line:5% position:50% size:92% align:center
The bulk is approximately neutral; voltage drops mainly across the two interfaces.

16
00:01:08.469 --> 00:01:12.437 line:5% position:50% size:92% align:center
Zoom into the semiconductor–electrolyte interface.

17
00:01:12.437 --> 00:01:15.696 line:5% position:50% size:92% align:center
The illustrated cation and electron layers are separated by about 1 nm.

18
00:01:15.696 --> 00:01:19.644 line:5% position:50% size:92% align:center
Here d is an effective charge-separation distance, not the electrolyte thickness.

19
00:01:19.644 --> 00:01:23.420 line:5% position:50% size:92% align:center
Very small charge separation can give a large capacitance per unit area.

20
00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:27.885 line:5% position:50% size:92% align:center
The ion layer acts like an effective gate charge layer close to the channel.

21
00:01:27.885 --> 00:01:30.971 line:5% position:50% size:92% align:center
Return to device scale and compare the two gate-control schemes.

22
00:01:30.971 --> 00:01:34.575 line:5% position:50% size:92% align:center
Use the same target: induce the same change in channel charge, ΔQ.

23
00:01:34.575 --> 00:01:38.178 line:5% position:50% size:92% align:center
Both channels accumulate charge together, but need different gate voltages.

24
00:01:38.178 --> 00:01:43.506 line:5% position:50% size:92% align:center
ΔV = ΔQ/C. With a larger effective capacitance, EDL gating needs less voltage.

25
00:01:43.506 --> 00:01:48.661 line:5% position:50% size:92% align:center
Illustrative example only: 100× capacitance would need 1/100 of the voltage change.

26
00:01:48.661 --> 00:01:53.471 line:5% position:50% size:92% align:center
For a fixed solid dielectric thickness, E ≈ V/d: higher voltage gives a stronger field.

27
00:01:53.471 --> 00:01:57.592 line:5% position:50% size:92% align:center
Prolonged high-field stress can lead to defects, leakage, and insulation degradation.

28
00:01:57.592 --> 00:02:00.161 line:5% position:50% size:92% align:center
A connected defect path illustrates gate-dielectric breakdown.

29
00:02:00.161 --> 00:02:06.351 line:5% position:50% size:92% align:center
Lower gate-voltage operation reduces reliance on high voltage and related failure risks.

30
00:02:06.351 --> 00:02:19.851 line:5% position:50% size:92% align:center
Limits: local interface fields can remain strong; intrinsic breakdown strength is not raised.
Electrochemical stability still matters. Source–drain high-field and heating failures remain possible.

31
00:02:20.694 --> 00:02:26.051 line:5% position:50% size:92% align:center
Ion redistribution → EDL → larger effective gate capacitance
→ less gate voltage for the same ΔQ → reduced reliance on high-voltage operation.
